00:00:49.996 --> 00:00:51.575
- [Voiceover] Tim asked, "If
you're not from an English

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:53.084
speaking country should
you produce content

00:00:53.084 --> 00:00:54.942
in English anyways, or should you produce

00:00:54.942 --> 00:00:56.701
content in your mother language?"

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.004
- Tim, that's a great question

00:01:00.004 --> 00:01:03.162
with a very simple answer, which is great,

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.832
because I'm answering a
boatload questions today.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.736
The answer is you should be
speaking in the language of

00:01:07.736 --> 00:01:09.175
the people you're trying to reach.

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:11.311
If you're trying to reach
the consumers in your native

00:01:11.311 --> 00:01:13.262
tongue, speak in your native tongue,

00:01:13.262 --> 00:01:15.677
unless English is also the second language

00:01:15.677 --> 00:01:17.998
that is covering let's say 80, 90% of the

00:01:17.998 --> 00:01:20.483
speakers, because you
get the serendipity if it

00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:22.921
goes outside the boundaries, but it always

00:01:22.921 --> 00:01:24.918
is reverse engineering the
language of your client.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:27.588
If you're trying to reach
English speaking consumers,

00:01:27.588 --> 00:01:29.167
then you gotta speak in English,

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:31.976
and again, if English is
enough of a second language

00:01:31.976 --> 00:01:34.345
with a big enough broad stroke according

00:01:34.345 --> 00:01:37.688
to the overall market,
that would be the reason

00:01:37.688 --> 00:01:38.756
the rationale around English

00:01:38.756 --> 00:01:41.351
otherwise it's a native tongue game.

